00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:06.720
In the last video, you saw what is unsupervised learning and one type of unsupervised learning

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:07.720
called clustering.

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:13.460
Let's give a slightly more formal definition of unsupervised learning and take a quick

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:17.920
look at some other types of unsupervised learning other than clustering.

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:23.800
Whereas in supervised learning, the data comes with both inputs x and output labels y, in

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:30.480
unsupervised learning, the data comes only with inputs x but not output labels y, and

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:35.500
the algorithm has to find some structure or some pattern or something interesting in the

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:36.500
data.

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:43.680
We've seen just one example of unsupervised learning called a clustering algorithm, which

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:46.320
groups similar data points together.

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:52.760
In this specialization, you learn about clustering as well as two other types of unsupervised

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:53.760
learning.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:01:00.200
One is called anomaly detection, which is used to detect unusual events.

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:05.840
This turns out to be really important for fraud detection in the financial system, where

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:13.480
unusual events, unusual transactions could be a science of fraud and for many other applications.

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:17.420
And you also learn about dimensionality reduction.

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:23.120
This lets you take a big data set and almost magically compress it to a much smaller data

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:27.320
set while losing as little information as possible.

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:32.280
In case anomaly detection and dimensionality reduction don't seem to make too much sense

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:34.240
to you yet, don't worry about it.

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:36.720
We'll get to this later in this specialization.

